now the families of those caught up in  the nineteen eighty four minus strike at  all grieve have demanded a full  investigation into allegations of police  brutality there the Home Office had said  it would announce whether or not there  would be an inquiry by the end of  October well here we are and they're  still be no word well Andy Burnham the  former shadow home secretary in  Manchester mayoral hopeful has been  campaigning for such an inquiry and  joins me now from his constituency in li  very good morning to you mr. Burnham so  uh not long left in October for this  decision do you do you think it's not  going to happen well no I hope it is  going to happen we need a decision and  we're expecting it tomorrow and in my  view the case for an inquiry is  overwhelming and undeniable now I've got  nothing but praise Dermot for the way to  resume handled hillsborough but you  can't be selective about which things  you look at and which things you don't  if you look at the evidence trail from  hillsboro it leads very directly to the  door of Orgreave this is the same police  force using the same tactics five years  earlier in the case of Hillsborough they  sought to deflect blame of the Liverpool  supporters after or grieve they did the  same with the miners so you know people  need the truth it's a reasonable request  and I make this last-minute appeal to  the government today please don't let  people plead for the truth they deserve  it order an inquiry into or grieve  tomorrow so that we can all understand  what happened draw a line and then  hopefully move forward and wide-ranging  I mean you mentioned the parallels with  Ellsbury I want to get onto south  yorkshire police again in a moment or  two but a similar kind at similar  configuration for the inquiry yes I  think so because Hillsborough in the in  the original phase had a panel that  simply disclosed the papers that were  held by the state Dermot and provided a  commentary as to how that disclosure  change things now the reason I'm  standing before this camera today and  talking to you as I promised those  Hillsborough families the full truth and  I don't think they can have the full  truth until we also know what happened  five years earlier in the aftermath of  all grieve it's the view of the chair of  the hills  Family Support Group Margaret Aspinall  that if South Yorkshire Police have been  held properly to account after or grief  or what they did then the cover-up over  Hillsborough might never have happened  and that is why this issue is so  important it's also important to be  honest though to heal the rifts that are  still there in mining communities one of  the the miners who was at Orgreave Kevin  thorne writes a very powerful piece in  the newspapers today saying that you  know he wants his grandchildren to trust  the police in the way that he was  brought up to do and if that is to  happen that does need to be this process  of reconciliation and what about South  Yorkshire Police we mentioned quite a  lot there in Hillsborough and previously  in or greed there are those now saying  well south yorkshire police should we  look at perhaps getting rid of them  amalgamating them with another force  well I think we need to have full  disclosure don't we about the past and  if necessary people held to account I I  pay tributes actually to the current  leadership the police and crime  Commissioner for his willingness to say  well we're ready to look at or grieve  and open up over or grieve but that that  needs to be done together with the  government so that we get the complete  picture it is crucially important isn't  it to the people of South Yorkshire that  we repair trust in their police force  I'm not necessarily calling for it to be  abandoned altogether but i do feel it's  unfair to those men and women who are  out there today on the streets of South  Yorkshire trying to keep people safe to  have these clouds of the past constantly  hanging over them the only way you can  get rid of those clouds is to say we're  going to have this inquiry we're going  to give people the truth and the justice  that they need and then hopefully as a  community we can move forward together  now mr. Burnham tell me about your  ambitions have become the mayor of that  well beefed up Manchester part of the  northern powerhouse extra money more  powers do you feel though that their  current administration I mean those  ambitions came namely from the previous  Chancellor George Osborne do you get a  sense the current administration is  cooling on the northern powerhouse a  little there's a lot of talk of the  Midlands engine these days I have  nothing against seeing the Midlands  regenerate too but  there were specific commitments made to  the northern powerhouse in the  conservative manifesto and in my view  they must be honored but also this  policy is is the right policy i pay  credit to george osborne for being  perhaps the first chancellor to talk  seriously about the north but the big  danger for the Tory party is they plated  a cravat clever slogan here with the  northern powerhouse but they may end up  seeing nothing behind it all spin and no  substance so yes we need to see the  government put their money where their  mouth is and they need to start actually  at the Autumn Statement you know we've  been trapped in this long debate on  Heathrow dermot which is kept the focus  on london and the south-east people  appear as saying what about us what  about transport in the north they're  still trundling on packed trains over  the Pennines it's not good enough so i  want to see a clear timetable in the  autumn statement for what it's called  hs3 east west high quality high speed  rail i would argue that is the single  highest transport investment priority  for this country linking the great  cities of the north I can't see how the  government can justify going ahead with  Crossrail to before that it's our turn  now surely and we need to see that  investment coming to the north of  England but on becoming a mayor of  Manchester you also need to see don't  you from the government the primary  legislation it hasn't been passed and I  was reading an interesting piece the  other day one of the blogs so many of  them I'm saying that why on earth would  a Conservative government that seems to  have nationally seems to have labor on  the run create these powerful mares in  northern power houses like Manchester  when they remember the lessons for  instance if Ken Livingstone and the old  GLC in the 1980s Margaret Thatcher got  rid of it because he was a thorn in her  side why would they create new thorns do  you think they will actually go through  with it that's an interesting question  isn't it but I hope they do because  actually it's the right policy that's  why they should go through with it it  wasn't actually conceived erm as the  answer to the referendum and brexit but  actually it should be embraced as such  now because what I think the referendum  result brought out was a cry for change  from many parts of the country that feel  abandoned that don't feel Westminster  has worked for them you know we've had a  situation in national politics where the  London perspective has dominated  national policy so when areas like mine  here wanted help with manufacturing  industry all of the national focus was  on the service sector when we wanted  help with affordable homes to rent  council housing all of the focus there  was on right to buy an owner occupation  and when we needed help with skills and  technical education the national focus  on the University route that's why  people feel the country hasn't been run  for them and that's why immigration  surely was a part two but of course  there's a deeper sense of of  disillusionment here so that's why the  government shouldn't abandon this they  should press on with it it's there it's  the time to deepen the commitment to  devolution in England and in me they're  not going to have somebody who is kind  of party political for the sake of it  picking fights I'm doing this I'm ready  to leave Westminster because I want to  devote myself to improving the lives of  people here rebalancing our country from  south to north that's why I'm doing it  if the government will work with me then  I will work with them well before you  leave Westminster what's your views on  the Richmond Park by-election you  mentioned Heathrow and we all know that  spark that by-election with zac  goldsmith there resigning to stand again  as an independent and there are those in  your party senior figures in your party  suggesting that Labour shouldn't stand a  candidate well because I haven't really  got much of a chance of winning it and  also to bolster the chance of the  Liberal Democrats to embarrass the  Conservatives my main view on it is it's  quite an indulgent act on the part of  zac goldsmith you know there's a pretty  significant price tag that comes with a  by-election and and what happened Dermot  to the old way of doing politics where  everybody debated things and had their  disagreements but when the party made a  decision that was the decision and  people then you know having had the  argument should have been there to  accept it so I kind of find it's a  distraction and actually again it will  it will keep all of the focus on  Heathrow on London on the southeast when  actually the focus should be up here  looking at the unacceptable transport  system that we have got so I personally  always take the view that labor should  contest by-elections we shouldn't give  the other parties of free run for narrow  tactical reasons but you know that's  just my that's just my view but my  overall I just think this is a huge  distraction when the country needs  baguette  the job of making sense of what brexit  means and arguing for a balanced  breakfast that protects the economy that  that's where our focus should be now not  having pointless by-elections got the  last question on that mr. Burnham I mean  flowing from the nissin a decision and  guarantees or not we don't know for sure  given to Nissan and its investment in  its Sunderland plant if you become mayor  of Manchester of course many many huge  exporters in and around the region who  will they be wanting would you be  fighting for for similar guarantees post  brexit that they wouldn't suffer extra  tariffs if it is a hard brexit  absolutely i mean i'm not against at all  the government helping a key company in  the north but they can't just do one  deal for them and leave everybody else  with nothing so the same surely Dermot  has to apply and you know we need to  know exactly what has been promised here  because there has to be a sense of  fairness doesn't to all companies in all  parts of the country and that's why I've  been arguing actually that as well as  Scotland Wales and Northern Ireland  having a seat at the table at these  brexit discussions Greater Manchester  needs one to Merseyside needs one west  and south yorkshire you know we can't  again have a situation where the London  perspective besides the terms of brexit  you know we need to hear the voices from  around the country so that we get a fair  brexit that works for everybody and I'll  be arguing very hard actually that a  hard brexit will hit the poorest parts  of the country the hardest of all I  challenged resume in the Commons about  this on last Monday and I said to her  you know has she looked at what the hard  brexit would do to the North West of  England and I said if she hasn't done  that analysis isn't it very  irresponsible to be burning our bridges  with Europe and talking up a harbor exit  and her reply was amazing because she  said I've never said I favor a hard  brexit what that tells me is she's  confused actually about where she's  going right now she's been ramping up  the rhetoric and actually when she went  to the European summit last week she  found Britain very isolated and very at  risk actually from from the rest of  Europe walking away from us so I think  the Prime Minister has got off to a bad  start on all of this and she needs to  get back to talking about a balanced  breakfast that yes responds to people's  concerns  on immigration but does not see our  economy sold down the river all right  and did you vote for you mate ed balls  last night I didn't son fortunately I'm  well impressed and his moves they were  never that good on the football field  that have to say I played up front  alongside him and he didn't move that  quickly then so I don't know what  they've been doing with him but it looks  impressive i have to say and you know  fair plates whimper doing it might be  you sometime you never know Andy Burnham  very good to talk very much as that's it  I view them

